data.content:
## What happened

In C# 14 / .NET 10, **overload resolution now prefers `MemoryExtensions.Contains<T>(this ReadOnlySpan<T>, T)` over `Enumerable.Contains<T>(this IEnumerable<T>, T)`** for array receivers, because `T[]` is implicitly convertible to `ReadOnlySpan<T>` and the span overload is considered more specific.

`ReadOnlySpan<T>` is a **ref struct** — it cannot be captured in expression trees or handled by the expression interpreter. So the expression tree either references a method the interpreter can't invoke, or the compiler emits an unsupported construct, causing the runtime failure.

## How to fix it

Force the compiler to resolve to `Enumerable.Contains` by casting the array to `IEnumerable<int>`:

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

// Option 1: Cast the receiver to IEnumerable<T>
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => ((IEnumerable<int>)array).Contains(num);

// Option 2: Call Enumerable.Contains explicitly
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => Enumerable.Contains(array, num);
```

Either approach pins the resolution to the `IEnumerable<T>` overload, which expression trees fully support. The same pattern applies to any span-preferring extension (`SequenceEqual`, `IndexOf`, etc.) used inside expression trees on array types.
